#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544948} :activity/id #uuid "2a2be071-2bca-52c8-92cb-23920fde7d03" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f44278-1870-59da-b693-ab5e9edbb7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1349" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544092} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbb0c6a-76c4-5add-8d12-dd2f98f7de84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1990" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544191} {:answer/id #uuid "59c37b7d-f2a8-5b03-96d5-27b94d5bddda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1909" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544285} {:answer/id #uuid "e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544580} {:answer/id #uuid "eee1f799-c2f5-5fbc-b0b8-69720cd98bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544580} :answer-field/id #uuid "74158e50-3525-5d26-bf97-113e74a7d386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132552" :question/problem "On a busy waterway, barges pass by a particular point continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ minutes. Determine the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2900fd92-b1ba-5748-8fdb-cafa51cae4b8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42155f4f-8bc0-57e6-9490-3cff23b40646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544234} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2c2bca-542a-586d-ad46-884f6485b4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544287} {:answer/id #uuid "774044da-9303-5308-898e-bc4f63b66972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544353} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c3fbd3-d9cf-5d4e-a641-4c48e70eef96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544471} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dc66d0-ea3e-5db7-9103-0b20306e7ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544591} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f736f9-b340-574e-a7c8-fcb3f016514e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-132566" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/31/314b881b95ea804456b3e41a1d8fec70cf459e007e2120d9018e3bef02f1b523.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1,\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y-1}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9b097164e2b5215a051e18de055f3cce4c26600caf510bc3b0071b77b645089.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29d93015-c02a-5f8e-8294-085ca82c8b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544099} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544366} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544512} {:answer/id #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} {:answer/id #uuid "e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" :question/math-academy-id "q-130722" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6746f8a6-ec2c-552a-9227-04e6ffc3fef5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844c9f0e-446b-5016-9b76-e7e445bdbd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461467} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc4d0a4-6d49-5f2e-aa11-b4018d2ef796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461467} :answer-field/id #uuid "8891a4fb-5ede-5445-a9fd-00d3e743bb62" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462487} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808eb96b-21d3-55b3-9334-bef1392eaad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461469} {:answer/id #uuid "58c883b2-35bd-528c-a6b1-d5516f9bd5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461469} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbd0ccb-df41-5cb9-b11a-d086bca693ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c96af62-46ae-56c7-88a0-22bb5ebc957b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461471} {:answer/id #uuid "a41e4398-f1c9-51f3-b2d3-cc1222c5d906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e4ca1a8-ea04-5785-aaa6-1097c70f54ba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c2f74bb-ddf4-5d4c-b244-ad5b6a694598" :question/math-academy-id "q-106897" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $10?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R)$, the vector space of all $5\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** ${R}^{10}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(\\text{R}))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5}(R))={5}^{2}=25\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7884f355-3021-5d3e-8e4b-bfc424f5de1d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f92bbf-f87d-57e4-a09f-bd3578ea1801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381120} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0911c8-0afc-5d04-a14b-c86b8755f154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381121} {:answer/id #uuid "d960268c-49c0-5bf5-8531-969a71b6d803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381122} {:answer/id #uuid "00742b97-d52c-53d6-8721-8e15c6bf04c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381123} {:answer/id #uuid "efea176e-ebef-5214-b3f5-e851df1e8e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381122} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d8b9da-2e10-512b-a08c-50b084b89d21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c035203-351a-592d-b1dd-a7ea354f996d" :question/math-academy-id "q-149711" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6y-x).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=6y-x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=6y-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{6} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "8044c493-8c36-5a5f-88c9-bddf70dfc3f4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3acc1c-34d4-53a3-a2c2-3bd4a879f97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(-19\\le Y\\le -17.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544114} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce4674c-5083-5b36-98a7-b915e1a9a375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544148} {:answer/id #uuid "25557390-cc90-5458-a850-eaa5fdae3391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(-18\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544170} {:answer/id #uuid "87bf84ce-9d23-5a06-9b73-ed4ab7fc1913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544380} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f9b909-0cca-5605-936d-c13ad832bc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(-19\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544148} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfef436-f0dd-5029-95f9-3d6c9dc629aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20" :question/math-academy-id "q-96576" :question/problem "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X=-18)$ can be approximated as" :question/worked-solution "We construct an interval around $X=-18$ with a radius of $0.5:$\n\n![](images/9c/9ccbf2335f4db52e60760d4740ebf29ac054b6b249b6f0436e02fbff3311d7d4.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=-18) & ≈P(-18-0.5\\le Y\\le -18+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aca56c02-4eed-569f-8354-82b4674f17d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5221f3d2-3945-59bf-8f2a-8074c4d35eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257481} {:answer/id #uuid "6335888e-8880-5dda-bb22-cb264ba5e045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257656} {:answer/id #uuid "65be3f91-2307-5b7c-a45f-3cfedff40838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257678} {:answer/id #uuid "90189be9-60a0-5430-b1cf-cb0e4429164f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258050} {:answer/id #uuid "cf076746-fa92-51fc-a391-045e00ab93d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258581} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb82f61-7d4c-5474-a0b0-ba491f22517e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4865ae47-4fd4-593e-a597-ec2a2d5aa88e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124276" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+7v+2,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-7y+3,\\,v=by+cx-2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+7v+2 \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+7v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-2)+(-7)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (x-2)+4\\cdot (y-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-7y+3 \\\\ 4y-x-2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-7y+3,\\,v=4y+(-1)x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+4+(-1)=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bfe077d4-9c8a-5d55-a2ea-96efc7434866" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544958}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544109} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9I+A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544286} {:answer/id #uuid "906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} {:answer/id #uuid "9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9I+3A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544423} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3I+9A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3364b42-0269-5bbd-853e-2a760baddc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1aab1c27-1f53-564d-b691-068d98d1a09c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37780" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )^{2}-15) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-9{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-9A+9I-{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =9I-9A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f65e260b-587e-5df4-9bc7-1971bc995d43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544912}}] :activity/title "Quiz 3" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544073}]}
